#
# Aelum's defaults for the Fcitx5 input method framework.
#
# The installer copies this file into the installed system (install/isocopy); the
# live session never reads it - it carries no fcitx5 at all. The list of input
# methods the key switches between is a separate file, profile beside this one,
# which the configure step writes from the language and location the install was
# given.
#
# Alt+Shift switches input methods. It is spelled with the Shift *keysym* because
# that is what the framework's parser reads: Key::Key in 5.1.23 looks for "Shift+"
# with a trailing plus, so the bare word at the end of "Alt+Shift" is dropped and
# the result is Alt alone, and a key with no keysym at all cannot be written in
# this file (Key::toString returns the empty string for one). Both Shift keys are
# listed so the key works from either hand.
#
# The Hangul and Zenkaku_Hankaku entries are upstream's own, repeated here because
# naming TriggerKeys replaces the whole list: they are the physical Hangul/Eng and
# 半角/全角 keys on Korean and Japanese keyboards, and dropping them would take a
# key away from exactly the machines that have it.
#
# Ctrl+Space moves from the toggle to enumerate. Toggling reaches English and the
# engine - that is the pair Alt+Shift is for - and on a Japanese or Korean install,
# which has its own keyboard as well, cycling is the only way to reach the keyboard.
#
# Nothing else is listed: fcitx5 loads this file on top of its own defaults and an
# option it does not find keeps the value it had, so every other setting stays what
# upstream ships - including Shift alone as the temporary switch.

[Hotkey/TriggerKeys]
0=Alt+Shift_L
1=Alt+Shift_R
2=Zenkaku_Hankaku
3=Hangul

[Hotkey/EnumerateForwardKeys]
0=Control+space
